1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Two computers, one wire, and four unrelated problems at once.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Solve each problem separately, and define one interface between neighbours.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Layer n talks to layer n on the other machine — logically. Physically, the message walks down and up.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Each step down adds a header. Each step up removes one. That is the whole mechanism.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
OSI has seven layers. The Internet stack has five. Both describe the same division of labour.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Application: the conversation. Transport: end to end, process to process.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Network: machine to machine across many networks. Link: one hop on one wire.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Four units of data: message, segment, packet, frame. A router reads two of them.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Four headers, one message. Each layer treats everything above it as payload.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Source and destination port: which program. Source and destination IP: which machines.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
At a router: strip the link header, choose the next hop, write a new link header. The packet is untouched.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
At the receiver the process repeats in reverse until only application data is left.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Circuit switching begins with a setup that reserves capacity on every link of the path.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Then data flows continuously along the reserved path. No switch ever stores it.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Teardown releases the reservation. The cost of the call is the setup plus the data time.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Guaranteed rate, no queueing, wasted silence. That is the circuit bargain.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Packet switching cuts the message into packets and sends each one when it is ready.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Store-and-forward: a switch receives the whole packet, then transmits it on.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Datagram: every packet is routed on its own, and two packets may take different paths.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Virtual circuit: a label is set up once, and every packet follows the same path.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Transmission delay: how long to push the whole packet onto the wire. Length divided by rate.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Propagation delay: how long one bit takes to cross the wire. Length divided by speed.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Queuing and processing delays sit at the switch. Unless the question gives them, take both as zero.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Rate times propagation delay is the number of bits that fit on the wire — the bandwidth-delay product.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
One packet, three links. It pays a transmission delay on every link it crosses.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
It also pays propagation delay on every link. Add the two sums, and that is the answer.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
GATE CSE 2022: 1000 bytes over 100 Mbps, 2100 km at 3 times 10 to the 8. The answer is 7.08 ms.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Notice that the packet size barely matters. On a long fast link, distance is the whole cost.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Send many packets back to back and the links start working at the same time.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The first packet needs every link. Each later packet finishes one transmission time after the one before.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
GATE CSE 2012: 1000 packets of 1000 bits over three links at 1 Mbps. Total 1005 ms.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
GATE 2024: 1000 chunks over two links. The second link adds one chunk time, so 8.008 s.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Smaller packets pipeline better but repeat the headers more often.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
GATE CSE 2014: one packet takes 3300 microseconds, ten packets 2400, twenty packets 3300 again.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Second trade-off: a circuit pays a setup once. Packets pay one packet time per extra link.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Throughput is set by the slowest link, and the bits in flight are set by rate times delay.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: write down L, R, d, v and P before touching any formula.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: one packet or many? That single question picks the formula.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: check which delays the question told you to ignore, and ignore only those.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Step four: sanity-check the units, then the magnitude, then the option.

